#13 15.03 
#13 15.03 This installation shell script will examine your system and ask you questions
#13 15.03 to determine how the perl5 package should be installed. If you get
#13 15.03 stuck on a question, you may use a ! shell escape to start a subshell or
#13 15.03 execute a command.  Many of the questions will have default answers in square
#13 15.03 brackets; typing carriage return will give you the default.
#13 15.03 
#13 15.03 On some of the questions which ask for file or directory names you are allowed
#13 15.03 to use the ~name construct to specify the login directory belonging to "name",
#13 15.03 even if you don't have a shell which knows about that.  Questions where this is
#13 15.03 allowed will be marked "(~name ok)".
#13 15.03 
#13 15.03 [Type carriage return to continue]  
#13 15.03 
#13 15.03 The prompter used in this script allows you to use shell variables and
#13 15.03 backticks in your answers.  You may use $1, $2, etc...  to refer to the words
#13 15.03 in the default answer, as if the default line was a set of arguments given to a
#13 15.03 script shell.  This means you may also use $* to repeat the whole default line,
#13 15.03 so you do not have to re-type everything to add something to the default.
#13 15.03 
#13 15.03 Every time there is a substitution, you will have to confirm.  If there is an
#13 15.03 error (e.g. an unmatched backtick), the default answer will remain unchanged
#13 15.03 and you will be prompted again.
#13 15.03 
#13 15.03 If you are in a hurry, you may run 'Configure -d'.  This will bypass nearly all
#13 15.03 the questions and use the computed defaults (or the previous answers if there
#13 15.03 was already a config.sh file). Type 'Configure -h' for a list of options.
#13 15.03 You may also start interactively and then answer '& -d' at any prompt to turn
#13 15.03 on the non-interactive behaviour for the remainder of the execution.
#13 15.03 
#13 15.03 [Type carriage return to continue]  
#13 15.03 
#13 15.03 Much effort has been expended to ensure that this shell script will run on any
#13 15.03 Unix system.  If despite that it blows up on yours, your best bet is to edit
#13 15.03 Configure and run it again.  If you can't run Configure for some reason,
#13 15.03 you'll have to generate a config.sh file by hand.  Whatever problems you
#13 15.03 have, let me (https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues) know how I blew it.
#13 15.03 
#13 15.03 This installation script affects things in two ways:
#13 15.03 
#13 15.03 1) it may do direct variable substitutions on some of the files included
#13 15.03    in this kit.
#13 15.03 2) it builds a config.h file for inclusion in C programs.  You may edit
#13 15.03    any of these files as the need arises after running this script.
#13 15.03 
#13 15.03 If you make a mistake on a question, there is no easy way to back up to it
#13 15.03 currently.  The easiest thing to do is to edit config.sh and rerun all the SH
#13 15.03 files.  Configure will offer to let you do this before it runs the SH files.
#13 15.03 
