#14 16.69 
#14 16.69 This installation shell script will examine your system and ask you questions
#14 16.69 to determine how the perl5 package should be installed. If you get
#14 16.69 stuck on a question, you may use a ! shell escape to start a subshell or
#14 16.69 execute a command.  Many of the questions will have default answers in square
#14 16.69 brackets; typing carriage return will give you the default.
#14 16.69 
#14 16.69 On some of the questions which ask for file or directory names you are allowed
#14 16.69 to use the ~name construct to specify the login directory belonging to "name",
#14 16.69 even if you don't have a shell which knows about that.  Questions where this is
#14 16.69 allowed will be marked "(~name ok)".
#14 16.69 
#14 16.69 [Type carriage return to continue]  
#14 16.69 
#14 16.69 The prompter used in this script allows you to use shell variables and
#14 16.69 backticks in your answers.  You may use $1, $2, etc...  to refer to the words
#14 16.69 in the default answer, as if the default line was a set of arguments given to a
#14 16.69 script shell.  This means you may also use $* to repeat the whole default line,
#14 16.69 so you do not have to re-type everything to add something to the default.
#14 16.69 
#14 16.69 Every time there is a substitution, you will have to confirm.  If there is an
#14 16.69 error (e.g. an unmatched backtick), the default answer will remain unchanged
#14 16.69 and you will be prompted again.
#14 16.69 
#14 16.69 If you are in a hurry, you may run 'Configure -d'.  This will bypass nearly all
#14 16.69 the questions and use the computed defaults (or the previous answers if there
#14 16.69 was already a config.sh file). Type 'Configure -h' for a list of options.
#14 16.69 You may also start interactively and then answer '& -d' at any prompt to turn
#14 16.69 on the non-interactive behaviour for the remainder of the execution.
#14 16.69 
#14 16.69 [Type carriage return to continue]  
#14 16.69 
#14 16.69 Much effort has been expended to ensure that this shell script will run on any
#14 16.69 Unix system.  If despite that it blows up on yours, your best bet is to edit
#14 16.69 Configure and run it again.  If you can't run Configure for some reason,
#14 16.69 you'll have to generate a config.sh file by hand.  Whatever problems you
#14 16.69 have, let me (https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues) know how I blew it.
#14 16.69 
#14 16.69 This installation script affects things in two ways:
#14 16.69 
#14 16.69 1) it may do direct variable substitutions on some of the files included
#14 16.69    in this kit.
#14 16.69 2) it builds a config.h file for inclusion in C programs.  You may edit
#14 16.69    any of these files as the need arises after running this script.
#14 16.69 
#14 16.69 If you make a mistake on a question, there is no easy way to back up to it
#14 16.69 currently.  The easiest thing to do is to edit config.sh and rerun all the SH
#14 16.69 files.  Configure will offer to let you do this before it runs the SH files.
#14 16.69 
#14 16.69 [Type carriage return to continue]  
